# Quantum-Inspired Intelligent Traffic Route Optimizer

## Setup

### Backend
cd backend
python -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate   # Windows: venv\Scripts\activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
python app.py

First run downloads the OSM road network for the city set in config.py
(default: Vijayawada, AP) and caches it as city_graph.graphml.
Change CITY_NAME in config.py for a different demo city — smaller
towns load faster than huge metros.

### Frontend
cd frontend
Just open index.html in a browser (or run: python -m http.server 8080)
Make sure the backend is running on http://localhost:5000 first.

## How it works
- graph_utils.py loads a real road network from OpenStreetMap
- traffic_simulator.py injects dynamic, time-varying congestion
- classical_router.py: Dijkstra + A* baselines
- qiga_router.py: Quantum-Inspired Genetic Algorithm — Q-bit chromosomes
  (rotation-angle amplitudes) are collapsed into concrete paths, scored,
  and evolved via quantum rotation gates + quantum NOT-gate mutation
- app.py exposes a Flask API; script.js renders everything on a Leaflet map
  and shows a live cost/time comparison between classical and quantum-inspired routes

Place `Banglore_traffic_Dataset.csv` here.

Download from Kaggle:
https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/preethamgouda/banglore-city-traffic-dataset

Expected columns (names are auto-normalized by traffic_simulator.py,
so minor spacing/punctuation differences in the CSV header are fine):

Date, Area Name, Road/Intersection Name, Traffic Volume, Average Speed,
Travel Time Index, Congestion Level, Road Capacity Utilization,
Incident Reports, Environmental Impact, Public Transport Usage,
Traffic Signal Compliance, Parking Usage, Weather Conditions

